            "text": "### 1. Product Identity\nThe application name **Gemini** and the endpoint `gemini.google.com` directly correspond. The URL `gemini.google.com` is the official web application endpoint for Google's conversational AI assistant, Google Gemini.\n\n### 2. Product Description\nGoogle Gemini is a web-based generative AI conversational assistant developed by Google. It allows users to query, summarize, brainstorm, analyze complex documents, draft content, and generate code using natural language prompts. When accessed by enterprise users signed in through managed accounts (such as Google Workspace or Gemini Enterprise), it can connect to organization data sources—including Gmail, Google Docs, Drive, and third-party tools—to assist with organizational workflows and information retrieval under enterprise administrative controls.\n\n### 3. AI Functionality\nArtificial intelligence is the **central, core functionality** of `gemini.google.com`, rather than an optional add-on feature. The entire interface and utility are built around interacting with Google's proprietary Gemini foundation models to handle tasks such as text generation, multimodal reasoning (processing text, audio, images, video, and code), summarization, conversational search, and agentic workflows. \n\n### 4. Alternate Names / Domains\n* **Bard / `bard.google.com`**: The application was originally launched as Google Bard on `bard.google.com`; in February 2024, Google rebranded the service to Gemini and redirected `bard.google.com` to `gemini.google.com`.\n* **Google Gemini / Gemini App**: Commonly referenced across Google documentation and consumer portals as \"Google Gemini\" or the \"Gemini app\".\n* **Gemini Enterprise / Gemini for Google Workspace**: The product is deployed for enterprise users under the Gemini Enterprise and Google Workspace product lines (formerly associated with \"Duet AI\" prior to the rebrand).\n\n### 5. Uncertainty\nThe web endpoint `gemini.google.com` serves both personal consumer Google accounts and enterprise-managed accounts (e.g., Google Workspace Business/Enterprise tiers). The exact features available (such as grounding against corporate drives or access to enterprise-grade data protection) depend entirely on the credentials of the logged-in user and cannot be determined from the endpoint URL alone.",
